00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:49.100
it's a real pleasure to be here i'd like to talk&nbsp;
today about ethics and data centric technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:55.740
but before i do i just wanted to offer a bit of a&nbsp;
warning i'm not a data scientist nor an engineer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:01.660
i don't code and honestly i more or less&nbsp;
use my computer as little more than a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:09.020
fancy typewriter i am going to be frank simply a&nbsp;
theorist who on occasion humbly attempts to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:15.180
about our contemporary condition of existence&nbsp;
and how we try to live within it so my talk today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:21.260
will be a theoretical intervention in the emerging&nbsp;
ethical concerns around data centric technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:27.580
put simply this is a theory paper i will leave&nbsp;
the practice to those who are able to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:35.020
and hope that they are inspired in their work in&nbsp;
what i say here today most of my talk is extracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:40.540
from an article called can machines be ethical&nbsp;
that i published about a year ago in the journal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:48.220
social research so you're welcome to go to that&nbsp;
journal and and and have a look to begin today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:54.220
though i will briefly make some superficial&nbsp;
distinctions of how ethics is generally talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.220 --> 00:05:00.860
about in relation to data centric practices and&nbsp;
technologies then i will continue by clarifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.460
what i mean by ethics as relational ethics&nbsp;
and finally i will provide an example that i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:12.300
hope illustrates that a relational ethics more&nbsp;
appropriately responds to the ethical concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.100
many have today about data centric&nbsp;
technologies and my less than hopeful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:23.180
outlook on the possibility of its&nbsp;
implementation so let's get started

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.180
ethics is a growing concern in the realm&nbsp;
of data sciences artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:37.340
and data centric technologies in general there&nbsp;
are good reasons for this we are all familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:43.580
with concerns over such issues as data privacy&nbsp;
or data-driven surveillance or the increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.700
intertwining of the data industry with the&nbsp;
finance industry or the so-called defense industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.300
we are all familiar with the fact that&nbsp;
data extracting and data-driven algorithms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:59.740
increasingly regulate the temporal affective&nbsp;
and inter-subjective modalities of everyday life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.780
and we are all familiar with how this&nbsp;
regulation along with the sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:11.740
over the top but sometimes legitimate concerns of&nbsp;
how ai may change the very definition of the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:17.100
as well as life itself is increasingly not&nbsp;
only of ethical but existential concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:23.100
indeed there are good reasons why we are&nbsp;
experiencing what i would call an ethical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:31.020
demand made by this data-centric situation we&nbsp;
now find ourselves in unfortunately at least in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:37.260
my view the response to this ethical demand by&nbsp;
most data-centric practitioners and ethicists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:43.980
is overwhelmingly framed in terms of principles&nbsp;
guidelines rules and ultimately law and policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:50.700
this response has taken various forms some for&nbsp;
example have called for increased ethical policing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:57.100
of data science research which might take the form&nbsp;
of institutional review board oversight others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:02.300
consider it vital that data scientists themselves&nbsp;
receive some form of ethical training to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:09.420
combat for example blindness to both their own and&nbsp;
structural biases that might enframe their work or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:16.380
to help combat what is now seen as the widespread&nbsp;
transgressions of data-centric industries finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.420
some have argued that data centric technologies&nbsp;
themselves such as artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:26.700
must be built with ethical capacities to help&nbsp;
guide their inevitable shaping of human worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:33.100
these are all reasonable and perhaps even&nbsp;
necessary responses unfortunately however and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:38.620
true to the instrumentalist reasoning that guides&nbsp;
so many data centric practitioners ethics in each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:44.300
of these various forms tends to be conceived in&nbsp;
terms of what i call either the checklist or rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:51.180
book approach to ethics what i call the checklist&nbsp;
approach considers a practice ethical if and only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:57.180
if certain predefined principles can be checked&nbsp;
off a pre-established list of such principles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:05.260
does a certain algorithmic practice respect&nbsp;
the principle of privacy yes good check ethical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.660
that in essence is the principle based or&nbsp;
checklist approach to ethics in action similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:17.980
the rulebook approach considers a practice ethical&nbsp;
if and only if pre-articulated rules are followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:25.180
have you guarded against the re-identification&nbsp;
of your data yes good rule followed ethical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.500
that in essence is the rule-based or&nbsp;
rule book approach to ethics in action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:34.380
while these two approaches may be useful&nbsp;
in certain institutional or bureaucratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.180
contexts for protection against litigation&nbsp;
or ethical research compliance for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:45.820
i argue they have nothing to do with ethics as a&nbsp;
human practice and the cultivated sensibilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:52.140
necessary to be and become an ethical person&nbsp;
therefore while the checklist and rule book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:58.380
approaches may be useful for example in the very&nbsp;
narrow concern of irb research approval or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.820
prevention of litigation it is the wrong road&nbsp;
to take if one were interested in for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:10.300
developing ethical persons who are also data&nbsp;
scientists or as i will focus on in the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:15.660
of this talk developing ethical data center&nbsp;
technologies such as artificial intelligence

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:23.260
in order to clarify this i will take a brief&nbsp;
detour to consider an early and important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:29.420
critique of artificial intelligence in&nbsp;
1965 the phenomenologist hubert dreyfus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:34.940
published a critical essay entitled alchemy&nbsp;
and artificial intelligence which was later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:41.820
expanded into the book what computers can't&nbsp;
do in short dreyfus was critical of the then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.100
dominant approach in artificial intelligence&nbsp;
research that assumed that human intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:53.580
is a matter of the mental representation of facts&nbsp;
and the abstraction and interiorization of rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:59.340
and as such researchers assumed that artificial&nbsp;
intelligence could be achieved through programmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:05.820
rules and the representation of facts this is&nbsp;
a symbolic information processing model of ai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:12.940
or symbolic a.i dreifus predicted rightly it&nbsp;
now seems that such an approach would fail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:18.860
as it began from the faulty cartesian assumption&nbsp;
of representationalism in contrast to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:24.220
cartesian approach to intelligence dreyfus&nbsp;
argued for an embodied and phenomenological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:30.380
approach to intelligence building on the work&nbsp;
of both martin heidegger and maurice marlo ponti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:36.540
dreyfus considered intelligence as primarily a&nbsp;
consequence of our embodied and involved ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:43.820
activity in situations in the world through which&nbsp;
over time we come to acquire not only background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:50.860
common sense understanding or intelligence but&nbsp;
also all the interests feelings motivations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:58.140
bodily capacities that go to make a human being&nbsp;
as such intelligence is a matter of an acquired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:04.620
and skilled habit that is continuously maintained&nbsp;
and developed by means of a kind of situationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:12.220
sensitive feedback loop in response to ongoing&nbsp;
and altering situations such embodied intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:18.300
stripis argued could never be represented by&nbsp;
data structures made up of facts and rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:24.380
as he went on to put it if the body turns out&nbsp;
to be indispensable for intelligent behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:30.300
then we shall have to ask whether the body can be&nbsp;
simulated on a heuristically programmed digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:37.260
computer if not then the project of artificial&nbsp;
intelligence is doomed from the start at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:42.940
time dreyfus's intervention was contemptuously&nbsp;
and publicly dismissed by most in the artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:49.500
intelligence community but privately many took it&nbsp;
seriously and considered how to adopt his insights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:56.620
today things have changed it seems rather obvious&nbsp;
that the contemporary successes of data-centric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.620 --> 00:12:02.380
machine learning are at least in part a matter&nbsp;
of these new technologies mimicking in some ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:06.140
the phenomenological approach to&nbsp;
intelligence that dreyfus argued for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:10.700
in his updated version of the&nbsp;
book what computers still can't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:17.980
dreyfus acknowledges that data centric ai and he&nbsp;
particularly focuses on neural networks has made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:23.500
some progress in this direction and more recently&nbsp;
he seems to have accepted that neural networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:29.820
have to a great extent satisfy the requirements&nbsp;
of this phenomenological approach to intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:37.020
founded on a kind of feedback adjustment between&nbsp;
mind and world importantly however dreyfus argues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:44.300
that the problem of generalization a key element&nbsp;
for intelligence cannot be solved without cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:49.980
be solved with a disembodied neural network though&nbsp;
it does indicate that a neural in that robot with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:57.100
a body may be able to solve this problem for now&nbsp;
however i will set this aside for the extent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:02.060
which dreyfus is correct in his analysis or&nbsp;
prediction is beyond the scope of this talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:08.380
what you may ask does this have to do with&nbsp;
ethics and what follows i would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:14.140
begin to convince you that contemporary concerns&nbsp;
with developing ethical data center technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:19.660
should make a similar shift in approach to a&nbsp;
phenomenological and relational notion of ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:26.620
the first point to make is this the checklist and&nbsp;
rule book approaches to ethics those approaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:33.020
that speak in terms of principles guidelines and&nbsp;
rules are analogous to the symbolic ai approach to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:39.100
intelligence of programming rules and representing&nbsp;
facts just as the ai community has learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:44.300
hopefully at least that intelligence and practice&nbsp;
is not a matter of symbolic representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:49.900
so too it must learn that ethics and practice&nbsp;
is not a matter of following rules or adhering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:56.060
to principles to put it bluntly ethics cannot&nbsp;
be pre-programmed as a set of rules or as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.060 --> 00:14:01.340
list of principles this approach outside&nbsp;
of a few institutional and bureaucratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:08.380
concerns is a dead end how then can we conceive of&nbsp;
ethics in a phenomenological and relational manner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:13.900
to begin to answer this in the next&nbsp;
section i will articulate in broad strokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:19.820
what i call relational ethics before in the&nbsp;
final section turning to a specific example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:24.700
of the ethical failure of a data centric&nbsp;
technology to illustrate the importance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:30.540
relational ethics not only for the future of&nbsp;
such technologies but for social life as such

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.300
for the next several minutes i will&nbsp;
indulge in a bit of philosophizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:43.180
so i ask your patients i hope though that&nbsp;
it will prove helpful as we move forward

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:49.340
perhaps the most fundamental of&nbsp;
all ethical questions is to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:55.500
how is it between us such a seemingly simple&nbsp;
question is immediately differentiable for more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.500 --> 00:15:01.900
commonly asked ethical questions such as what&nbsp;
is the good or did she act rightly and in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:09.100
differentiation its radicality is revealed when&nbsp;
we ask what is the good or did she act rightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:15.020
there is an assumption built into these questions&nbsp;
that there is a the good the wetness of which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:22.140
can we can query or an exact rightness that&nbsp;
can come to modify precisely a particular act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:28.700
when we ask these questions we assume the&nbsp;
prior existence of the good or the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:33.740
already there as the object or the measure&nbsp;
in reference to which the questions could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:41.420
answered definitively oftentimes the good or the&nbsp;
right are articulated in terms of rules principles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:47.180
guidelines and laws the pre-establishment of&nbsp;
which stand available as that against which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:54.540
can measure our ethical adequacy is it possible to&nbsp;
conceive of ethics without the good or the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:59.740
always already there to measure up to the&nbsp;
standard by which any possible ethics is judged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:05.180
in the rest of this section i will respond to&nbsp;
this question in the affirmative and begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:11.980
offer in broad strokes an outline for what i call&nbsp;
relational ethics as that which offers a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:19.340
to the query of how it is between us the work of&nbsp;
the philosopher immanuel lebinos is vital here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:23.900
in contrast to all of those ethical&nbsp;
theories that make the ontological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:30.220
assumption that human subjects exist as&nbsp;
ethical subjects with some sort of a priori&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:37.340
built-in capacity to act ethically for example&nbsp;
to be rule followers or moral calculators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:43.900
or autonomous law givers and adherents lebinos&nbsp;
argues that the ethical subject is constituted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:51.820
through what he calls the other what does this&nbsp;
mean allow me to oversimplify the other always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:58.700
exceeds any idea that i may have of that other&nbsp;
for example in terms of what and who the other is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:05.340
what the other desires or even how i should&nbsp;
act with and toward the other and loving us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:11.740
names this phenomenon of the excess of the&nbsp;
other infinity because i and the other are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:18.940
always intertwined in this infinite relationality&nbsp;
as an ethical subject i must give myself over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:24.300
or open myself to the unknowable and&nbsp;
incomprehensible difference that is the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:31.180
therefore and in contrast to reducing the other&nbsp;
to comprehensibility through a proceduralist act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:39.260
i have what levines calls infinite responsibility&nbsp;
to the other perhaps more appropriately rendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:46.620
i must respond to the call or demand of the&nbsp;
other or alternatively i must attune to the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:53.180
momentarily i will return to this concept of&nbsp;
responsivity and show how lebinos considers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.180 --> 00:18:00.940
the form of conversation as the structure&nbsp;
of this attuned response for leaving us not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:06.540
only the possibility of ethics but the very&nbsp;
possibility that i become an ethical subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:12.700
begins with the other this formal structure&nbsp;
of the condition of possibility for ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:18.940
and the ethical subject is what lebanese calls&nbsp;
transcendence this notion of transcendence does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:24.940
not establish a radical dichotomy between&nbsp;
ordinary everyday life and some realm beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:31.660
and it does not necessitate religion or some other&nbsp;
sphere of values neither it should be noted does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:38.220
levinoxian transcendence indicate an imminent&nbsp;
urge to transcend any given option toward its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:45.020
presumably better state or alternative rather&nbsp;
it simply expresses the relational structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:51.980
of ordinary everyday existence or as lebinos puts&nbsp;
it the ecstatic relationality of transcendence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:58.620
delineates the structure of exteriority and&nbsp;
thus makes possible the pluralism of society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:04.540
perhaps the ordinariness of relation&nbsp;
of this relationality is best revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:10.460
but lebinos's insistence that it begins in the&nbsp;
bodily materiality of the face of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:19.500
confronted by the enigma of the face of the&nbsp;
other i must respond i must attune when this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:25.740
attunement does not occur in a smooth fashion the&nbsp;
enigma of the other makes an ethical demand on me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:32.380
the experience of which is what i have called a&nbsp;
moral breakdown far from the mischaracterization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:38.140
of the rupturing of the everyday made by those&nbsp;
who have critiqued this concept of moral breakdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:44.300
the experience of a moral breakdown is in fact&nbsp;
more intensely felt and considered relational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:50.540
intertwining with others and this relationality is&nbsp;
more intensely felt and considered in the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:56.620
of breakdown precisely because the demand of the&nbsp;
other has explicitly called me to ethically attune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:03.020
in the next section i will consider more closely&nbsp;
how this attunement as empathic attunement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:09.660
is the very condition of possibility for ethics&nbsp;
despite the significance of this relational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:15.740
structure for considering ethics i have argued&nbsp;
elsewhere that the asymmetry that levinas posits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:22.620
falls short of the kind of mutuality of beings&nbsp;
some of us might prefer to consider if for loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:27.980
us the other is separated from me by what he&nbsp;
calls an infinite curvature of intersubjectivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:34.620
then my task is to conceive of this asymmetrical&nbsp;
relation in more mutual terms without reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:40.940
this mutuality to a totalized co-relational&nbsp;
pseudo-relation that is our question might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:47.660
posed as this how to maintain the enigma of the&nbsp;
other and the asymmetrical relation this entails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:52.460
while at the same time recognizing&nbsp;
that the other must also relate to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.460 --> 00:21:00.140
as an enigmatic other put another way how can we&nbsp;
conceive mutuality without reducing this relation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:07.340
to a totality of the same i will begin to address&nbsp;
these questions by turning to what i call between

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:17.420
how is it between us in contrast to asking the&nbsp;
op priority question indicated by the what or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:22.860
did a question that begins with the assumption&nbsp;
that ethics is an accomplishment of a telos or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:30.380
acting according to a predefined measure to ask&nbsp;
how is to ask about an ongoing process imminent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:37.820
situations within which we find ourselves to ask&nbsp;
how is to understand ethics as ongoing attunement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:46.780
who attunes us in contrast to the individual&nbsp;
who aims for the good or attempts to act rightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:54.300
and in contrast to a we that is a totalized&nbsp;
or categorized plurality the us that attunes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.300 --> 00:22:00.620
is the non-categorizable plurality of singular&nbsp;
beings that are neither individuals nor merely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:08.540
a part of a collective but unique instances&nbsp;
of being relational put in lebanosian terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:13.500
the non-categorizable us is&nbsp;
indicative of a relational ontology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:19.660
that entails that the other is always infinitely&nbsp;
other to me as well as other possible others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:28.140
how is it between us this ongoing attunement&nbsp;
imminent to worlds and situations is indicative of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:34.860
the ecstatic revelationality that gives way to us&nbsp;
the place of this giving way i will name between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:42.140
but do so with the caveat that this between that&nbsp;
is relationality neither can be objectified thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:49.340
like nor spatialized through this nomination&nbsp;
and yet this between the relationality emerges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:57.820
how phenomenological analysis would disclose&nbsp;
multiple responses to this question but given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:04.140
the limitations of this talk today as well as&nbsp;
its clear resonance with many ethical concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:10.220
raised by data-centric technologies i will focus&nbsp;
on one possible response and that is language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:15.180
the phenomenological hermeneutic&nbsp;
conception of language is helpful here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:19.580
a good starting point for understanding&nbsp;
an important version of this is martin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:25.660
heidegger's claim that language is the house&nbsp;
of being what could such an enigmatic phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:33.420
mean when he wrote this he immediately followed&nbsp;
it with this quote in it in its home man dwells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:41.260
dwell on language and do so as existing for&nbsp;
heidegger as it is for much of this tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:49.180
to exist is to be ecstatic always to be outside&nbsp;
oneself intertwined with other ecstatic beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:56.220
human and non-human alike for humans language&nbsp;
is one of the primary entanglements of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:02.940
relationality thus it is to a great extent through&nbsp;
language that humans are pulled beyond themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:10.380
to dwell in between with others notice this&nbsp;
phenomenon is a pulling and not a projecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:15.820
unlike those conceptions of language that&nbsp;
begin with a view of language grounded in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:21.740
prior and already established set of criteria or&nbsp;
grammar that is projected out from the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:28.140
speaker a phenomenological view of language&nbsp;
sees language as a primary effective force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:36.060
emanating from between us that pulls and places&nbsp;
demands on us to respond the manifold responses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:42.460
which manifest more as listening than speaking&nbsp;
enliven the between by means of their distinction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:48.940
thus in contrast to the projection of&nbsp;
an assumed shared criteria or grammar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:54.460
a projection that levinox considered to be an&nbsp;
act of violence the distinctive and manifold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.460 --> 00:25:01.820
responses to the call of language give way to a&nbsp;
between characterized above all by difference a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:08.860
difference that is to which attunement is ongoing&nbsp;
this is simply another way of describing ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:15.660
hydra can heidegger contrasted this view of&nbsp;
language with more common conceptions of language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:21.100
which as he put it is the notion that language&nbsp;
is the expression produced by humans of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:27.180
feelings and the worldview that guides them as he&nbsp;
went on to claim this is a notion of language from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:33.100
which we must become liberated this is the notion&nbsp;
that assumes not only that language is structured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:40.300
by a set of shared criteria and grammar but also&nbsp;
can express a shared meaning elsewhere i have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:45.660
argued against the anthropological focus on shared&nbsp;
meaning and have tried to make the case that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:50.700
least in ethical encounters although i presume&nbsp;
this is indeed the case with most encounters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:55.900
language does not express shared&nbsp;
meaning but rather acts as what i call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.900 --> 00:26:02.860
a bridge of being that is to say ethics is&nbsp;
ongoing attunement is not about adhering to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:09.420
pre-established criteria or grammar and neither is&nbsp;
it about finding the spot of shared meaning rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:16.540
the extent that language is a modality of ethical&nbsp;
attunement it is that call that demand that pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:23.180
that allows the possibility to dwell once&nbsp;
again again with others in the world between us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:29.180
language as a modality of ethics&nbsp;
then calls us places a demand on us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:35.420
to be with others in the world in an attuned&nbsp;
manner letting us vote of this phenomenon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:41.420
for loving us language is the relation&nbsp;
between the same and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:47.820
which is just a fancy philosophical way of saying&nbsp;
me and you whoever the me and the you might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:55.580
ethically this relation takes the form of&nbsp;
conversation here it is important to emphasize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.580 --> 00:27:01.420
that loving us is not arguing that the actual act&nbsp;
of conversation is ethics though at times it may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:07.660
be but rather that the form of conversation can&nbsp;
be understood as the formal structure of ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:16.780
what does this mean in conversation lavinas writes&nbsp;
we become astonished through conversation with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:24.220
other the other through conversation the other is&nbsp;
revealed in her strangeness to me i can never know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:31.180
prior to the other speaking what she will say&nbsp;
and as such i must listen and then respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:37.740
but i cannot respond with a projection my&nbsp;
response cannot be a claim of knowing the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:41.500
and neither can it be a taking over&nbsp;
the conversational relation with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:47.900
claim that for example ah yes the same thing&nbsp;
happened to me because the same thing never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:53.820
happens such a claim is not a continuance of&nbsp;
open conversation but rather shuts it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.820 --> 00:28:00.860
through the colonization of the other by me in&nbsp;
conversation the other is revealed is not the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:07.100
which means that she cannot be thematized through&nbsp;
a projection of an all priori criteria or grammar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:12.860
or the claim of a shared experience or the&nbsp;
fitting into some preconceived principle or rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:21.260
rather to be in conversation is to let the other&nbsp;
be and then attune as such relational ethics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:27.580
encounters the alternative of the other through&nbsp;
a tuned letting be that preserves the between of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:34.380
difference if as the anthropological linguist&nbsp;
eleanor oaks has written ordinary enactments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:40.380
of language are modes of experiencing the world&nbsp;
this can only be so because worlds and situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:47.820
are already partly constituted through language&nbsp;
constituted not as i've been trying to argue as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:54.860
worlds in situations of a projected shared meaning&nbsp;
or a shared form of life but rather as the between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.860 --> 00:29:00.140
that pulls us together in our differences in&nbsp;
the modalities of concern and content care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:07.980
how is it between us this is perhaps the&nbsp;
most fundamental of all ethical questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:14.780
because as humans we can only live together in&nbsp;
worlds and situations as intertwined in a between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:21.260
that is all we can ever share it is for this&nbsp;
reason that the philosopher hannah arndt argued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:27.420
that we are concerned and cared for the between&nbsp;
because we have an interest in its being for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:35.740
between is essential to the human condition we are&nbsp;
concerned and care in our interest in our interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:43.180
that is in our being between as being&nbsp;
relational if in 1958 hannah arndt could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:48.780
limit the between to the human condition in&nbsp;
the 21st century that is no longer possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:55.980
today such phenomena is climate change and the&nbsp;
colonization of everyday life by data center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.980 --> 00:30:02.140
technologies has made it abundantly clear that the&nbsp;
between is essential to the existential condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:09.660
the between can no longer be limited to between&nbsp;
you and me but must be the between of existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:16.220
and yet the manner in which the human responds&nbsp;
to the call of this existential between will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:22.940
be decisive do we continue to project our already&nbsp;
established rules and principles onto the between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:31.100
or do we let be and attune if how is it between&nbsp;
us is the most fundamental question then how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:36.780
respond to it must be the most fundamental&nbsp;
of ethical as well as existential replies

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:48.460
i have just articulated in broad strokes a&nbsp;
relational ethics that begins from the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:54.780
humans are primarily beings who are relationally&nbsp;
intertwined with others in specific situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.500 --> 00:31:00.940
and that what counts as ethics in our everyday&nbsp;
lives is not a matter of adhering to a checklist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:07.020
of principles or following a rule book but&nbsp;
rather responding and attuning to situationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:12.060
unique demands placed upon us in&nbsp;
the ongoing vicissitudes of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:18.220
in this sense ethics is not a matter of deciding&nbsp;
between a number of predetermined options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:24.140
but rather in each instance entails a&nbsp;
singular response to this situation now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:31.420
such a response takes the form of an attunement&nbsp;
not only to the singularity of the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:37.100
but to the singularity of the other or others&nbsp;
there with you and to do so in the modality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:43.260
of letting be and care in the rest of my talk i&nbsp;
will illustrate the importance of this notion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:48.780
relational ethics for considering the possibility&nbsp;
of ethical and data centric technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:52.940
and most importantly their relational&nbsp;
intertwining in our everyday lives

00:31:56.380 --> 00:32:02.780
consider the tragic case of mark hemmons as&nbsp;
recounted by john jamie lippeld in his book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:10.220
we are data this is a quote from&nbsp;
the book it's 2013 and mark hemmings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:17.980
a 41 year old british disabled man dials the&nbsp;
number for emergency services in the uk 999&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:26.780
an operator answers and hemmings immediately makes&nbsp;
his condition known my stomach is an agony duck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:32.700
i've got lumps in my stomach the operator&nbsp;
responds with the first of many inquiries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:40.620
have you got any other symptoms hemming replies&nbsp;
i'm in agony sweating a lot feeling weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.620 --> 00:32:48.140
going on mostly all day the operator asks more&nbsp;
questions have you had any diarrhea or vomiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:53.340
have you ever been diagnosed with an aortic&nbsp;
aneurysm or marfan syndrome have you got any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:59.260
crushing or aching pain in your upper abdomen&nbsp;
through the agony hemmings diligently puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:07.340
hemings diligently but excruciatingly answers them&nbsp;
all he requests in the ambulance three separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:14.940
times he also explains his pain and description&nbsp;
and accompanying tone as agonizing a heartbreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:21.100
seven times at the end of the call the operator&nbsp;
concludes from what you've told me you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:27.820
require an emergency ambulance but we will get&nbsp;
somebody to call you back 90 minutes later a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:33.500
general practitioner did call mr hemmings and&nbsp;
recognize that he needed to be seen immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:40.780
still he refused to make a house call and&nbsp;
told mr hemmings to take an ambulance instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:48.060
mr hemmings told the gp that he had been refused&nbsp;
an ambulance nevertheless and not taking this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:54.140
important information into account the gp still&nbsp;
insisted that mr hemmings come into the hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:59.180
and made an appointment for him when mr&nbsp;
hemmings did not show for the appointment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:06.700
his records were marked as did not attend two days&nbsp;
later mr hemmings was discovered by an in-house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:12.780
healthcare worker unconscious in his home and died&nbsp;
minutes after having been rushed to the hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.660 --> 00:34:20.220
mr hemmings died of a heart attack brought on by&nbsp;
gallstones blocking his pancreatic duct a routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.220 --> 00:34:24.940
operation would have saved his life if he had been&nbsp;
correctly diagnosed and brought to the hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:28.940
although the official cause&nbsp;
of death was heart attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:34.780
it is perhaps more accurate to say that the tragic&nbsp;
case of mr hemings is one of death by algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:38.780
but the questions asked by&nbsp;
the operator were not her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.340 --> 00:34:45.180
nor were they read from some template but rather&nbsp;
came directly from an algorithmic triage system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:50.860
the human operator simply acted as the&nbsp;
mouthpiece the voice of the algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.220 --> 00:34:57.980
what can we learn from this tragic case the most&nbsp;
obvious takeaway is that an ethical relation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:04.380
not simply a matter of input and output although&nbsp;
relational ethics is as i argued in the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:11.100
section an ongoing process of responsibility or&nbsp;
attunement is not simply a feedback mechanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:18.620
mr hemmings provided all of the input he was asked&nbsp;
to but the output was not ethically adequate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:24.940
his situation neither obviously was it medically&nbsp;
adequate but i will set aside that disturbing fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:31.900
i should note that almost certainly a linear&nbsp;
algorithmic triage system was used in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:38.300
which has an essential task ensuring that certain&nbsp;
rules are followed thus it would be inappropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:43.180
to expect that it could attune to the singularity&nbsp;
of mr heming's ethical demand for care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:50.620
as no delineated rules ethical or otherwise&nbsp;
could allow for attunement input of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:58.140
nature is simply illegible to such an algorithmic&nbsp;
system some argue that a non-linear triage system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:02.780
based on machine learning and other artificial&nbsp;
intelligence elements would have the capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:09.340
to respond to the singularity of individual&nbsp;
medical needs this may be so and yet this does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:15.020
not entail that such a system would be any better&nbsp;
responding to the singularity of ethical demands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:21.340
for ethical demands are not a matter of providing&nbsp;
more data and ethical responses are not a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:28.220
of adjusting parameters to increase data&nbsp;
let's be clear the ethical demand made by mr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:34.940
hemmings in his 999 call was not for the most part&nbsp;
articulated through his factual description of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:42.540
symptoms but rather by means of his interjected&nbsp;
affective utterances sounds and tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.540 --> 00:36:49.340
that infuses answers to the algorithmic questions&nbsp;
mr hemmings did not simply provide facts but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:56.300
importantly was making an ethical demand on&nbsp;
another mr hemming's ethical demand is not made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.300 --> 00:37:02.540
in his description of his bowel movements but&nbsp;
rather when he several times says i'm in agony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:08.940
or asks if we could have an ambulance and&nbsp;
speaks the entire time in an agonizing tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:14.780
with interspersed sounds that indicate severe&nbsp;
pain suffering and the vulnerable need for care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:20.860
in other words mr heming's ethical demand&nbsp;
is not made through the articulation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:27.100
facts read as data that may or may not fit the&nbsp;
measurable type of deserving emergency treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

